## Tools beyond the editor

Out of the box, Cursor's Agent can read your files, edit code, and run terminal commands. But real development touches the outside world: your database, your GitHub repos, your documentation, your browser. MCP, the Model Context Protocol, is the standard way to give the Agent those powers.

Think of an MCP server as a translator. It sits between Cursor and some external service and exposes that service's abilities as tools the AI can call. Install a GitHub MCP server and the Agent can read your issues and open pull requests. Install a database MCP server and it can inspect your tables. Install a browser MCP server and it can take screenshots of your app and check its own visual work.

## Set up your first MCP server

MCP servers are configured in a file called `.cursor/mcp.json` in your project root. Each server is a small JSON block describing how to launch it:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
      "env": {
        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "your-token-here"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Most MCP servers run via `npx`, which downloads and launches them on demand, so you usually do not install anything separately. After saving the file, open Cursor Settings, go to the MCP section, and enable the server. You will see its available tools listed, things like "create issue" or "list pull requests."

A word of caution that matters: that token in the config is a real credential. Never commit `.cursor/mcp.json` with real tokens to a public repo. Use environment variable references where the server supports them, and add the file to `.gitignore` if it contains secrets. The never-commit-secrets rule from every security guide applies here too.

## What to connect first

For most builders, three MCP servers cover 90 percent of needs. A GitHub server connects your repos, issues, and pull requests, so the Agent can work from a real issue description. A database server (Postgres, Supabase, and others have official ones) lets the Agent inspect schemas and test queries safely. A fetch or web server lets it pull in documentation and API references without you pasting them.

Add servers one at a time and test each before adding the next. After enabling a server, ask the Agent something that requires it: "list my open GitHub issues" or "show me the tables in the database." If it works, the Agent will call the tool automatically. If it fails, check the MCP settings panel for error messages, which usually point at a bad token or a launch problem.

## The trust boundary

Every MCP server extends what the Agent can do, which also extends what it can break. A database tool that can read tables might also be able to write to them. Before enabling a powerful server, check which tools it exposes and whether they are read-only or read-write. Cursor lets you approve tool calls, and for destructive tools you should keep approvals on.

Start with read-only servers while learning. The GitHub issue reader before the pull request creator. The schema inspector before the query runner. You can always grant more power later, and you will appreciate the caution the first time the Agent misunderstands a vague instruction near a production database.
